      "content": "That's much better, \"curated haven\" works well. \n\nI'm curious about the linguistic difference you're creating between the two. When you wrote the Oak Ridge one, you used words like \"legacy,\" \"refinement,\" and \"tranquility.\" For the Heights, you're using terms like \"exciting strata,\" \"cultural currents,\" and \"dynamic.\"\n\nIf we were to present these two properties side-by-side in a brochure, how would you say the \"vibe\" we're projecting for each differs? Like, what kind of person are we subconsciously describing for the Oak Ridge house versus the one in the Heights? I want to make sure we aren't accidentally making one sound \"old money\" and the other sound \"trendy/temporary.\"",
